ingest: refuse roots structurally, walk three levels, drop the count prompt

A 57-session drop sat blocked for eleven hours at 2.7 seconds of CPU. The
sidecar had reached Confirm.ask("Continue?") - the >16-session guard - on
stdin it inherited from the Xcode console, so it was waiting on a terminal
nobody could see. No terminus event, sidebar still showing it as running.

The guard is gone rather than fixed, because count was never the right
axis. A thousand ten-second video answers to one question is an ordinary
unmoderated study; ten ninety-minute interviews is five times the work at
one percent of the file count. It blocked the cheap case and waved the
expensive one through. It also fired after the scan it was meant to gate,
so it never protected the part that costs anything.

What it looked like it was protecting - someone dropping a filesystem
root - is now handled where it belongs. _refuse_reason rejects filesystem
and system roots by name before anything touches the disk. Measured
first: a whole-filesystem walk finds 5 media files in 0.3s, so depth was
never the hazard either.

Depth 1 -> 3, because real folders nest: project/week1/zoom1/video.mp4.
This removes Zoom's special-casing rather than deepening it - the Zoom
rule keys on the containing directory name, so it works at any depth and
zoom1/ becomes just a folder that happens to hold media.

Two resilience fixes found while measuring. Dropping a home folder raised
PermissionError out of iterdir on .Trash and destroyed the entire scan -
one bad entry killing everything, the same shape as the zero-byte file
that aborted a 42-session run the night before. And discovery probed
duration on every media file, which materialises cloud placeholders
first: scanning a folder of evicted recordings downloaded all of them to
learn their length. Ten such files sit within three levels of this home
directory. Duration at scan time is a nicety; the bytes are genuinely
needed at transcription, where the wait is expected and attributable.

Defence in depth: the sidecar's stdin is now /dev/null, so no prompt
anyone adds upstream can ever block a headless run again.

Verified: 4016 passed, 83 skipped, 22 xfailed; ruff clean; corpus
acceptance unchanged at 53 ingested / 5 refused / 0 dropped / 0 aborts.

